(* ============================================================================
   30. SECTIONING IS INTERCHANGE-ONLY -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-052]. A `section`
      carries `children`, the blocks it encloses, and MAY carry `level`, the
      heading level the source format stated for it.

      `section` is interchange-only (CARVE-P12-066): Carve's own sectioning is
      the implicit heading-level kind of CARVE-P9-019. The canonical writer
      flattens one back to its headings and reports `structure-unspellable`
      for `section` on the CARVE-P11-046 channel.
      The fallback matrix row `section` gives HTML, Markdown, plain and ANSI
      output. HTML emits one `<section>`, merging `attrs` under ordinary
      block-element rules and rendering its children as blocks. Markdown, plain and ANSI emit children in document order with
      no wrapper and report nothing. `level` renders on NO target: it records
      the source format's stated level, while the enclosed heading is visible.

      THE DERIVED WRAPPER DOES NOT DOUBLE. PART 9 §13 walks TOP-LEVEL blocks and
      a `section` is a container, so a heading inside one is not top-level: it
      emits no derived `<section>` and keeps its id on the `<h*>`, exactly as a
      heading in a blockquote or a div does. A reader sees ONE wrapper, the
      authored one, whether CARVE-P9-019's `sections` option is on or off, and a fragment
      URL resolves to the heading's id the way it does under `sections: false`.

      IT EXISTS BECAUSE THE FORMATS ON THE OTHER SIDE OF A BRIDGE HAVE IT:
      HTML5, JATS, DocBook, DITA and TEI importers can preserve explicit sectioning.
      THE LEVEL IS WHAT THE SOURCE SAID, NOT WHAT THE NESTING IMPLIES. A format
      stating a level publishes it; otherwise the field is absent and nesting
      supplies the answer. They cannot disagree in an importer-built tree, so
      readers need not check
      ([carve#2207](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2207)).

      A CAPTION IS NOT A NODE, and the profile vocabulary no longer says it is.
      `figure`, `figure_group` and `table` each carry their caption as an
      inline array, so there is nothing a profile could deny separately from
      its host. Listing it gave implementations a name that named nothing.

   31. A CITATION ITEM CARRIES ITS OWN MODE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-053].
      A `citation` MAY carry `mode`, its citation mode. Absent means
      parenthetical. `citation_group.mode` is the AUTHORED shorthand that sets
      it: the leading `+` after `[` is the only spelling Carve 0.1 source has,
      and it applies to the whole group, so every item of a source-spelled
      group carries the same value as its group.

      IT SITS ON THE ITEM BECAUSE THE EXCHANGED MODEL PUTS IT THERE. Pandoc's
      `CitationMode` is three-valued and per citation, so a `Cite` mixing an
      author-in-text citation with a parenthetical one had no encoding while
      the mode lived only on the group. The group keeps its field, because
      that is what the author wrote and CARVE-P12-005 records the document rather than a
      rendering of it.

      NO CORPUS DOCUMENT MOVES. A parse from Carve source fills every item of
      a group identically from the group's own value, so the only trees where
      the items differ are ones an importer or an editing API built. A
      canonical Carve writer cannot spell such a group and reports the loss.

      A READER MUST REFUSE A GROUP WITH `mode` WHEN ANY ITEM LACKS IT. The
      schema rejects that contradiction. Without group `mode`, items MAY carry
      different modes; each item's value remains authoritative. No source-spelled
      group has mixed item modes
      ([carve#2203](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2203),
      [carve#2251](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2251)).
   32. RUBY ANNOTATIONS SURVIVE INTERCHANGE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-054]. A
      `ruby` inline node carries `pairs`, a non-empty ordered array. Each pair
      is a closed object with exactly `base` and `annotation`: `base` is a
      non-empty array of inline nodes and `annotation` is an array of inline
      nodes which MAY be empty. The ruby node MAY also carry `attrs` and `pos`
      under the ordinary inline-node rules. It has NO parallel `children`
      field; the two arrays are what preserve the association.

      PAIRS ARE STRUCTURE, NOT NODES. They carry no `type`, `attrs` or `pos`.
      Their order is reading order, and every traversal walks `base` before
      `annotation` in pair order. Sanitization, URL policy, profile enforcement,
      resolution, cloning, equality, patching and rendering MUST reach both
      arrays. One `ruby` counts as one inline nesting level. AST ingest still
      measures depth in each implementation's own unit under CARVE-P12-028(c).
      A consumer that ingests a conforming serialized `ruby` node MUST
      reproduce the node and every pair unchanged when it serializes that tree
      again.

      Ruby is interchange-only (CARVE-P12-066). The fallback matrix row `ruby` gives target output. The canonical writer
      replaces each pair with base, text `(`, annotation, text `)`, then writes
      and escapes that sequence under CARVE-P11-002 and CARVE-P11-006. Empty annotation stays
      visible as `()`. With `attrs`, the writer wraps the complete fallback in
      one ordinary attributed span. It reports `ruby-flattened` under PART 2. The source round-trip
      compares against this flattened sequence. The writer MUST NOT discard
      either side.
      HTML emits pairs in order inside one `<ruby>`, with generated `<rp>(</rp>`
      and `<rp>)</rp>` around each `<rt>`; the outer element receives `attrs`.
      Plain and ANSI emit `base(annotation)` and report `ruby-flattened`.
      CARVE-P11-045 defines Markdown output.

      PROFILES USE THE EXISTING `disallowedAction`. For `to_text`, the text
      content is each base followed by `(`, its annotation and `)` in pair
      order. `strip` removes the node and subtree; `error` rejects it. Ruby is
      not a SemanticSpan, an `inline_extension` or a span carrying magic
      attributes. A later source spelling MUST map to this node rather than
      introduce a second representation.

   33. A BLOCK EXTENSION DECLARES A CORE FALLBACK -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-055]. A `block_extension` carries a globally qualified
      `name`, a REQUIRED `fallback` block, and MAY carry `version` and
      `payload`. It is interchange-only (CARVE-P12-066).

      IT IS THE TWIN `inline_extension` NEVER HAD: Carve-content payloads can
      use a div or admonition; non-Carve payloads need this declared home (CARVE-P12-030).
      THE FALLBACK IS REQUIRED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
      DESIGN: it carries meaning for readers that do not implement the extension.
      The fallback matrix row `block_extension[unknown name]` applies to them.

      THE PAYLOAD IS NOT CARVE CONTENT. `format` names how `value` is
      encoded so a reader can tell whether it can parse it at all. No core
      target renders it, CARVE-P12-028's ingest bound covers its nesting like any other,
      and the security rules of CARVE-P9-054 cover it like any other content a
      renderer did not produce.

      A NAME IS QUALIFIED so two extensions cannot collide. `diagram` is not a
      name; `org.example.diagram` is.

      A READER THAT DOES NOT KNOW THE NAME RENDERS THE FALLBACK AND REPORTS
      THE LOSS. It does not refuse the tree: an unknown extension is not an
      unknown field, and the node states its own degradation. Where the envelope
      marks the extension `required`, refusal happens at that boundary (CARVE-P12-056),
      before reading the tree
      ([carve#2200](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2200)).
   34. A STORED TREE IS WRAPPED IN A VERSIONED ENVELOPE -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-056]. At a STORAGE OR PROCESS boundary a Carve AST is carried
      inside an envelope validating against
      `resources/ast-envelope-schema.json`: `astVersion` and `document`
      required, `vocabulary` and `extensions` optional, nothing else.

      THE TREE DOES NOT MOVE. `document` is exactly what PART 12 describes and
      `ast-schema.json` pins. `carve --json` still writes that tree bare, an
      in-memory handoff still passes it bare, and every clause of this PART
      applies to it unchanged. The envelope wraps; it does not amend.

      WHY IT EXISTS: the closed tree (CARVE-P12-030 and CARVE-P12-031) cannot distinguish a corrupt
      payload from an unsupported contract, vocabulary or required extension.
      The envelope lets a reader name the failure.

      `astVersion` IS THE CONTRACT'S VERSION, NOT THE LANGUAGE'S. It is
      `major.minor` and it does not track the Carve version: the language is
      versioned for authors and this is versioned for readers of a tree. A
      MAJOR bump removes, renames or reinterprets a field or a meaning; a MINOR
      bump adds a field or a node type. The current contract is 1.2. A leading
      zero is refused so the 0.x reading of a major never applies here.

      WHAT 1.2 ADDS, all optional on read, nothing removed, renamed or
      reinterpreted:
        - `resolved: true` on a link or image reference use whose matching
          definition has an empty destination (CARVE-P12-006). Its `href` or
          `src` is `""`; without the field an empty one is unresolved, as in 1.1.
        - `astVersion` on the root of a bare tree (CARVE-P12-024), below.
        - `form` on `comment`: `"block"` for `%%%`, `"line"` for `%%`,
          `"braced"` for `{% … %}`. `block` and `delimited` stay. A writer
          emits all three; a reader refuses a node where they disagree, with
          the CARVE-P12-068 error: `block` is true exactly when `form` is
          `"block"`, and `delimited` is true exactly when `form` is `"braced"`.

      A MINOR SAYS WHAT UPGRADING COSTS, NOT WHAT AN OLD READER TOLERATES. It
      tells a reader's author that moving up means handling new fields and
      nothing else, and it tells a producer that every reader of the same minor
      or higher reads its trees. IT DOES NOT SAY A LOWER READER COPES, which
      (b) below spells out.

      WHAT A READER DOES:

      (a) A HIGHER MAJOR IS REFUSED, with a typed error naming the version it
          got and the version it implements. Not a schema failure: the payload
          may be perfectly well-formed under a contract this build predates.
      (b) A HIGHER MINOR IS REFUSED TOO, with the same typed error naming both
          versions. A minor adds a FIELD, and THE SCHEMA STAYS CLOSED below --
          `CARVE-P12-030` rejects any property the schema does not name -- so a
          1.0 reader handed a 1.1 tree fails on the field itself, as a schema
          error that names no version. Accepting the envelope and then refusing
          its payload is the worse of the two failures, not the gentler one.
          THE READER ACCEPTS ITS OWN VERSION OR LOWER. Weakening the ingest
          instead is the answer CARVE-P12-030 already ruled cannot be right
          ([carve#2199](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2199)),
          and this clause's own reason for existing is that the reader need not
          be weakened to evolve.
      (c) AN EXTENSION MARKED REQUIRED AND NOT IMPLEMENTED IS REFUSED, named.
          Rendering the understood parts and reporting success is what
          CARVE-P12-068 forbids.
      (d) A MISSING `vocabulary` MEANS THE CORE ONE. A profile that denies
          types does not change it: a denied type is one the document does not
          use, not a different vocabulary.

      A PRODUCER EMITS ONE CANONICAL VERSION - the contract its build
      implements - rather than echoing what it read. Re-emitting an ingested
      `astVersion` would republish a claim the producer cannot keep.

      A BARE TREE MAY CARRY ITS VERSION ON THE ROOT. `carve --json` writes
      `astVersion` on the root of the tree it prints, so a reader of bare trees
      can detect the contract version. A reader applies (a) and (b) to it and
      accepts its absence: a bare tree without one is read against the
      reader's own contract, and a closed schema then reports any mismatch as
      an unnamed property rather than as a version. An in-memory handoff need
      not carry it.

      THE SCHEMA STAYS CLOSED. `additionalProperties: false` holds on the
      envelope and on every node inside it. Versioning solves evolution without
      weakening the ingest, and CARVE-P12-030 already ruled that a pass-through is the
      answer that cannot be right
      ([carve#2199](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2199)).

   35. A NAMED CONTAINER IS A CALLOUT, A DIRECTIVE OR A DIV -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-057]. An ordinary `:::` container produces one of three
      types after the reserved openers have been recognized. Bare `::: figure`
      produces `figure_group`; `::: |`, `::: \`, and `::: >` produce their
      own block types (PART 2). An ANONYMOUS or attribute-only ordinary
      container is a `div`. A named one whose kind names
      GENERATED CONTENT - `bibliography`, `footnotes`, `glossary`, `index`,
      `references`, `toc` - is a `directive`. Every other named container is
      an `admonition`.

      WHY THE SPLIT: consumers can dispatch on `type` without keeping their
      own list of generated-content kinds that are not callouts.

      `admonition.kind` REFUSES the six kinds, so the split is checked and not
      only stated: one thing has one spelling
      ([carve#2231](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2231)).

      FOLDING THEM INTO `div` WAS REFUSED: a profile can deny admonitions
      while keeping generic containers; a fold would remove that ability.

      AN EXTENSION-DEFINED CONTAINER KEEPS ITS QUALIFIED NAME and declares the
      base it generalizes to, so a processor that knows only the base can still
      handle it - the same reading the block extension clause takes for a payload it
      cannot parse.

      A DIRECTIVE ALWAYS PUBLISHES `children`, with `children: []` when no
      blocks were written between the markers. Its content is usually generated
      from the document; authored blocks are carried. PART 9 §16 governs the
      body of a core `footnotes` directive. Tier-3 extension contracts govern
      their own bodies. This is the same empty-container shape as `admonition`
      ([carve#2195](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2195),
      [carve#2333](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2333)).

      A DIRECTIVE MAY CARRY A `title`. The opener admits a quoted title on
      every named container, so `::: toc "Contents"` spells one: the field is
      optional and holds that text as authored inline content, exactly as
      `admonition.title` does. The DISPATCH DOES NOT MOVE - the kind alone
      decides the type, an untitled directive is unchanged, and a titled one
      renders its title and label under PART 9 §12
      ([carve#2247](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2247)).

   36. A LINE BLOCK MAY PUBLISH ITS LINES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-058]. A
      `line_block` MAY carry `lines`, parallel to `children`: entry `i` holds
      the LINES of the stanza `children[i]`. `children` is unchanged and still
      required.

      A LINE IS A RANGE, NOT A SEQUENCE. Each entry is an array of RFC 6901
      pointers relative to its stanza, one per line and in document order,
      naming where that line STOPS: the `hard_break` that closes it, or
      `/children/-`, the past-the-end pointer, for the last line. Line `n` is
      the content between pointer `n-1` and pointer `n`, excluding both, so a
      run spanning a boundary is neither re-bracketed nor duplicated and no
      half is synthesized.

      A `hard_break` NO POINTER NAMES IS AUTHORED CONTENT. Below, the boundary
      sits inside the `strong` and the trailing `\` closes nothing, so the
      stanza has two lines and publishes
      `"lines": [["/children/0/children/1", "/children/-"]]`:

        ::: |
        *Roses are red
        Violets are blue*\
        :::

      `lines` DOES NOT SPAN STANZAS. A blank line ends a stanza, `children`
      keeps that boundary, and no line crosses one, so a consumer reading one
      entry is reading one stanza's lines.

      PRESERVED INDENTATION IS UNAFFECTED. A leading run of non_breaking_space nodes is per line
      either way, and PART 12 §4's exact-source-range rule governs it.

      ABSENT, THE LINES ARE WHAT SPLITTING `children` ON `hard_break` YIELDS,
      which over-counts a trailing break and under-counts a boundary inside a
      run. Where both are present they cannot disagree in a tree a parser
      produced, and `lines` is the finer statement
      ([carve#2235](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2235)).

   37. A CITATION IS ONLY EVER AN ITEM OF A GROUP -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-059].
      The inline dispatch does NOT name `citation`. The type stays declared,
      and `citation_group.items` references it directly, so nothing about a
      group's payload moves.

      A CITATION IS NOT AN INDEPENDENT INLINE. PART 7 makes the bracket the
      unit a source spells -- `citation_group = '[', ['+'], citation_item,
      {';', citation_item}, ']'` -- and an item's `prefix`, `locator` and
      `suffix` mean nothing outside one. No clause defines what a bare
      citation renders to in any target, because no target was ever asked.

      ALL THREE ENGINES ALREADY AGREE: their inline unions admit only
      citation groups. CARVE-P12-038's typed, positioned item and inline-profile vocabulary
      requirements remain satisfied by the `citation` definition and vocabulary
      entry. THE COST OF LEAVING IT WAS NOT SYMMETRIC: narrowing the dispatch
      lets CARVE-P12-068(d) reject standalone citations at ingest instead of at rendering
      ([carve#2227](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2227)).

   38. NODE IDENTITY IS A SEPARATE OPT-IN SIDECAR -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-060]. A
      producer MAY return ephemeral node ids beside an AST, as a sidecar under
      CARVE-P12-067.

      THESE ARE NOT `attrs.id`. That id is what the author wrote and belongs to
      the document; this one is machinery and belongs to the session. A
      producer MUST NOT derive either from the other.

      The sidecar validates against `resources/ast-node-identity-schema.json`.
      Its `version` is 1, and each entry of `nodes` binds an `id` to a `path`.
      Both are unique within a sidecar.

        {"version":1,"session":"s-7f3","nodes":[{"id":"n4","path":"/children/3"}]}

      THE PATH IS THE BINDING, NOT THE IDENTITY. A pointer moves when a sibling
      is inserted before it, so it holds only for the tree the sidecar came
      with. A producer re-emits the whole sidecar after an edit, carrying the
      SAME `id` at the new path for a node it still recognizes; a consumer MUST
      NOT carry a path across an edit.

      AN ID THE EDIT INVALIDATED IS ABSENT, not tombstoned. A node the producer
      no longer recognizes has no entry in the next sidecar, and that absence
      is how a consumer learns its anchor is gone. An id is never reused for a
      different node within one `session`.

      A re-parse after a canonical write mints fresh ids, and `session` is the
      opaque token naming the minting a sidecar belongs to: a consumer MUST NOT
      match ids across two sidecars whose `session` values differ.

      NO SPAN IS RESTATED. A node's offsets are `pos` on the node the path
      addresses, in codepoints per PART 12 §4; a second copy could disagree.

   39. A COUNT WITH NO MARKERS IS INGESTED AS THE SPAN -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-061]. A reader that meets a `table_cell` carrying `colspan` or
      `rowspan` with no continuation cell over the positions it covers DERIVES
      those positions from the count. Against a well-formed grid the covered
      positions are a function of the counts, so inverting the span walk of
      CARVE-P9-002 is a derivation and not a guess. An HTML or Pandoc tree arrives
      this way because neither format HAS a continuation cell for a bridge to
      have written.

      THE MARKERS WIN WHERE A TREE CARRIES BOTH AND THEY DISAGREE. A
      continuation cell's `span` is the document record under CARVE-P12-005; a count is a
      resolution result under PART 12 §5, and a derived value never overrides the value
      it was derived from. CARVE-P12-048 already calls such a payload invalid; this says
      which half a reader that accepts it keeps.

      REFUSAL IS FOR ARITHMETIC, NOT FOR A MISSING MARKER. A count reaching
      past the table's last row or column, or one whose positions another
      cell's count already occupies, has no derivation: that is a malformed
      tree, refused under CARVE-P12-068. A count
      with no markers is an interchange shape, and nothing refuses it.

      AN ORPHAN MARKER IS NOT EXPRESSIBLE THIS WAY. CARVE-P12-048 publishes a marker that
      found no origin carrying `span` and moving no count, so a count-only tree
      cannot state one. Accepted: a format with no continuation cell has no
      marker to orphan, and a tree that carries markers keeps them.

   40. AN ANNOTATION RANGE IS A SEPARATE OPT-IN SIDECAR -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-062]. A producer MAY return annotation ranges beside an AST,
      as a sidecar under CARVE-P12-067: the inline tree nests and a range need
      not.

      The sidecar validates against
      `resources/ast-annotation-range-schema.json`. Its `version` is 1, and each
      entry of `ranges` carries an `id`, a `kind`, a `start` and an `end`, and
      MAY carry `data`, the holder's payload, which core never reads. An
      anchor is a `path` and an `offset` in codepoints per PART 12 §4 from the start of
      that node's annotation text, defined below. An offset is a boundary from
      zero through the annotation text's codepoint length, inclusive. Readers
      MUST reject an out-of-range offset or a path that is not a node.

      ANNOTATION TEXT IS A DETERMINISTIC PROJECTION, not rendered output or a
      source slice. A node contributes its first string field in this order:
      `value`, `content`, `text`, `alt`; absent means the empty string. A
      soft_break or hard_break contributes one U+000A instead, and a
      non_breaking_space contributes one U+00A0. No other scalar field,
      attribute, resolved label, URL or opaque extension payload contributes.

      Append child-node projections in the following field order, independent
      of JSON object member order:

        target, title, children, items, rows, cells, blocks, inline, content,
        prefix, locator, suffix, old, new, pairs, base, annotation, caption,
        shortCaption, fallback

      Only node-bearing positions named by node-roles.json are traversed.
      Arrays retain their order; ruby pair records additionally traverse base then annotation.
      A scalar content field is counted once, never traversed again. No implicit
      separator is inserted between child nodes or blocks. Each node's interval
      starts before its own contribution and ends after its descendants; global
      offsets in the root projection determine whether start follows end.
      Thus a paragraph containing text, an image, a break and math counts the
      text, the image's alt, one newline, and the math source, in that order.
      Emoji count by Unicode codepoints, not UTF-16 units or grapheme clusters.

        {"version":1,"ranges":[{"id":"c-12","kind":"comment",
        "start":{"path":"/children/3","offset":4},
        "end":{"path":"/children/4","offset":9}}]}

      RANGES OVERLAP FREELY. Two ranges may cross, and either may cross a node
      boundary; nothing re-brackets the tree to make one nest, which is the
      capability the sidecar exists for. `start` never follows `end` in
      document order, and a range whose anchors are equal is the empty point a
      cursor is.

      `kind` IS AN OPEN VOCABULARY, matched by equality, and core defines no
      member of it: a closed list would name kinds no clause renders. One core
      did not define is globally qualified as an extension name is in
      CARVE-P12-055.

      AN ANCHOR THE EDIT INVALIDATED IS ABSENT, not tombstoned, exactly as CARVE-P12-060
      rules an id: the producer re-emits the whole sidecar against the new tree
      and drops a range it can no longer place, and a consumer MUST NOT carry a
      path across an edit.

      `id` is the holder's, not `attrs.id` and not a CARVE-P12-060 node id, and a holder
      keeps it across a canonical write, which the anchors do not survive
      ([carve#2232](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2232)).

   41. PROVENANCE IS A SEPARATE OPT-IN SIDECAR -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-063]. A
      producer MAY return provenance beside an AST, as a sidecar under
      CARVE-P12-067.

      The sidecar validates against `resources/ast-provenance-schema.json`.
      Its `version` is 1. `sources` names each input once - an `id`, an optional
      `uri`, an optional `format` and an optional `parent`, the id of the
      source that included or imported it. `nodes` binds a `path` to a
      `source`, an `origin` of `authored` or
      `generated`, an optional `step`, the transformation that produced the
      node, and a `startByte` and an `endByte` in THAT input. The two bytes
      come as a pair; a generated node has no range and omits both.

        {"version":1,"sources":[{"id":"s1","uri":"ch1.md","format":"markdown",
        "parent":"s0"}],"nodes":[{"path":"/children/3","source":"s1",
        "startByte":40,"endByte":68,"origin":"authored"}]}

      ANCESTRY IS ON THE SOURCE, NOT THE NODE. A nested include reaches its
      root by walking `parent`, recorded once per input instead of copied onto
      every node that came from it. A root input has no `parent`, and CARVE-P6-001
      already refuses a cycle.

      A BYTE RANGE IS NOT AN OFFSET. The two bytes count the input they name
      (CARVE-P12-067); PART 12 §4's codepoint rule governs `pos`. When `pos.file` is present, every `pos` coordinate is measured in
      that named input file; otherwise it is measured in the top-level input.
      There is no implicit assembled-document coordinate space. Byte ranges
      and pos can address the same input but use different units. Containment
      comparisons apply only to positions naming the same input. A producer
      converting pos to bytes MUST use that input's text, never the parent's.

      THIS IS WHERE A FILESYSTEM PATH MAY APPEAR. `pos.file` stays the inline
      minimum per PART 12 §4, and a `uri` a caller did not ask for is one it never
      receives. An ABSENT `format` is unrecorded, not Carve, because CARVE-P12-032 reads
      a missing fact as unknown and never as a guessed default.

      PROVENANCE IS PER NODE. A range has no independent existence in the tree,
      so CARVE-P12-062's holder reads the nodes its anchors address
      ([carve#2233](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2233)).

   42. A CONTINUATION LINE IS A MARKER IN THE JOINED CELL -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-069]. Where a `+` continuation row joins non-empty content
      onto a cell (PART 9 §5 T6), the parser puts a `cell_wrap` node in place
      of the single joining space. `cell_wrap` carries `line`, the number of
      the row's continuation line that part came from, counted from 1. The
      marker sits wherever the join falls in the parsed tree, so it may be a
      descendant of a span the two lines share:

        | *bold | x |
        + text* | y |

        table_row{ continuationLines: 1, cells: [
          table_cell[ strong[ "bold", cell_wrap{line:1}, "text" ] ],
          table_cell[ "x", cell_wrap{line:1}, "y" ] ] }

      `table_row.continuationLines` counts the row's `+` lines, and is absent
      when there are none; it keeps a line whose cells are all empty, which
      leaves no marker. A cell with nothing on line k has no marker for k.
      Its part on line k is what lies between its markers for the lines
      before and after; what precedes the first marker is the standard row's
      own part. A cell carrying `span` has the marker itself as its standard
      row's part, so its children begin on the `+` line (CARVE-P12-048).

      UNDER A ROWSPAN the joined content belongs to the SPANNING cell (T6), so
      the marker goes there too, and it adds `row`: how many rows below the
      cell's own row the `+` line follows. `line` then counts that row's
      continuation lines. `row` is absent when it is 0.

        | a | b |
        | ^ | c |
        + more | d |

        table_row{ cells: [ table_cell{ rowspan: 2,
          children: [ "a", cell_wrap{line:1, row:1}, "more" ] }, ... ] }
        table_row{ continuationLines: 1, cells: [
          table_cell{ span: "rowspan" },
          table_cell[ "c", cell_wrap{line:1}, "d" ] ] }

      UNDER A COLSPAN the fragment on a `<` joins the cell the `<` widens (T6),
      so one cell can take two parts from one `+` line. Each marker then adds
      `col`: how many columns right of the cell's left edge the part was
      written. `col` is absent when it is 0, and an atomic node's `cellWraps`
      entry carries it the same way.

        | a | < |
        + x | y |

        table_cell{ colspan: 2, children: [ "a", cell_wrap{line:1}, "x",
                                            cell_wrap{line:1, col:1}, "y" ] }

      AN ATOMIC INLINE NODE CARRIES THE JOIN AS AN OFFSET. When a join crosses
      a `code`, `raw_inline`, or `math` payload, the node carries an optional
      `cellWraps` array. Each entry is an object `{ offset, line, row?, col? }`:
      `offset` is the codepoint offset (PART 12 §4) of the single joining
      space in `value` for `code`, or `content` for `raw_inline` and `math`,
      and `line`, `row` and `col` mean exactly what they mean on a `cell_wrap`.
      Offsets are ascending and each names a space inside the payload,
      except where code-span padding stripping removed the joining space:
      that entry sits at the padding boundary, offset 0 for a join right
      after the opening backtick run and the value's length for one right
      before the closing run
      ([carve#3138](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/3138)). A
      `cellWraps` entry stands for the join a `cell_wrap` would otherwise
      mark; no `cell_wrap` is placed beside or inside the atomic node for
      that join. Rendering is unchanged, and `table_row.continuationLines`
      still counts every join.

        | `a | z |
        + b` | w |

        table_cell[ code{ value: "a | z b",
                          cellWraps: [{offset:5, line:1}] } ]

      A cell with nothing on a `+` line has no entry for it, so `line` says
      where the remainder goes:

        | `a |
        + |
        + b` |

        table_row{ continuationLines: 2, cells: [
          table_cell[ code{ value: "a b",
                            cellWraps: [{offset:1, line:2}] } ] ] }

      A MARKER IS NOT A SECOND TREE. The cell's `children` stay the one parsed
      inline tree; literal per-line child lists are refused because the parts
      are joined BEFORE inline parsing (CARVE-P2-019 item 2), so a span that
      crosses the line could not be split. Every renderer and writer other
      than the Carve writer outputs `cell_wrap` as one space, the joining
      space it replaces, so no HTML, Markdown, plain or ANSI output moves.
      Normalization never merges text across a `cell_wrap` and never drops
      one.

      THE CARVE WRITER WRITES THE LINES BACK. It cuts each cell at its
      markers and cuts atomic payloads at their `cellWraps` entries, putting
      each part on the `+` line its `line` and `row` name, in the column its `col` names. It writes
      `continuationLines` `+` lines after the row, each
      holding the matching part of every column, empty where a cell has no
      part. A `+` line is spelled like a standard row with `+` for its first
      pipe, so an empty part is the single space of any empty cell:

        | Complex | A long description |
        + | that continues |

      A `table_row` with no `continuationLines`, such as an imported
      one, is written on one line. A `cell_wrap` outside a `table_cell`, a
      `line` above the `continuationLines` of the row it names, a `row` equal
      to or above the cell's `rowspan`, a `col` equal to or above its
      `colspan`, or two markers in one cell with the same `line`, `row` and
      `col` (a `cellWraps` entry counts as a marker here), or
      a `cellWraps` entry whose offset is not ascending, does not name a space
      inside the payload and is not a padding-boundary entry, or sits on a node outside a `table_cell` is invalid in the sense of CARVE-P12-030
      ([carve#3094](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/3094)).

   ============================================================================ *)
